[Event "Knight School"]
[Opening "Sicilian Najdorf"]
[Variation "Sicilian Defense: Najdorf Variation, Opocensky Variation"]
[ECO "B92"]
[Character "solid,positional"]
[Risk "low"]
[EarlyMate "none"]
[Tricks "none"]
[Themes "central-control,piece-activity"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6
6. Be2 e5 { Kicks the knight away from the centre. White's quiet bishop move leaves Black free to take the middle of the board. }
7. Nb3 (7. Nf3 Nbd7 { Black develops and keeps the e5 pawn well supported. })
7... Be7 { Develops the bishop and prepares castling. }
8. O-O O-O 9. Be3 (9. f4 Nc6 { Black attacks the pawn in front of the knight, so White's centre starts to loosen. })
9... Be6 { Develops the last bishop on a diagonal that hits the knight on b3. }
10. f4 (10. Qd2 Nbd7 { The knight develops and guards the e5 pawn. } 11. Rad1 Qc7)
10... exf4 { Takes the f4 pawn, so White cannot squeeze Black with f5 or fxe5. The bishop on e6 and the d-pawn get more room. }
11. Bxf4 Nc6 { Develops with an eye on ...d5 and ...Ne5. White's pieces have no natural way to stop both. }
12. Qd2 b5 { Starts the queenside expansion; the a6 pawn guards the new pawn on b5. } *
